## Proof

**Claim:** For every integer $n \geq 1$, there exists a compact complex manifold of complex dimension $n$ that is holomorphically parallelizable, i.e., admitting $n$ holomorphic vector fields forming a basis of the holomorphic tangent space at every point.

**Construction (complex torus).** Let $\omega_1, \dots, \omega_{2n} \in \mathbb{C}^n$ be $\mathbb{R}$-linearly independent vectors, and let
$$\Lambda = \mathbb{Z}\,\omega_1 + \cdots + \mathbb{Z}\,\omega_{2n} \subset \mathbb{C}^n$$
be the full lattice they generate. Define
$$X = \mathbb{C}^n / \Lambda.$$

**$X$ is a compact complex manifold.** The lattice $\Lambda$ acts on $\mathbb{C}^n$ by translations $z \mapsto z + \lambda$ ($\lambda \in \Lambda$). This action is free (no fixed points), properly discontinuous (each $\lambda \neq 0$ moves every point by at least $\min_{\lambda \in \Lambda\setminus\{0\}} \|\lambda\| > 0$), and by holomorphic automorphisms. Hence the quotient $X$ inherits a complex structure from $\mathbb{C}^n$, with $\pi: \mathbb{C}^n \to X$ a local biholomorphism. Compactness follows because $X$ is the continuous image of the compact fundamental parallelepiped $\{\sum t_i \omega_i : 0 \le t_i \le 1\}$. The complex dimension of $X$ is $n$.

**$X$ is holomorphically parallelizable.** Let $z_1, \dots, z_n$ be the standard holomorphic coordinates on $\mathbb{C}^n$, and consider the constant holomorphic vector fields
$$V_i = \frac{\partial}{\partial z_i}, \qquad i = 1, \dots, n.$$
These form a global holomorphic frame for $T\mathbb{C}^n \cong \mathbb{C}^n \times \mathbb{C}^n$.

**Key invariance.** For any $a \in \mathbb{C}^n$, the translation $\tau_a(z) = z + a$ has derivative $d\tau_a = \mathrm{Id}$ at every point (its Jacobian matrix is the identity). Therefore
$$(\tau_a)_* V_i = V_i \quad \text{for all } a \in \mathbb{C}^n, \; i = 1, \dots, n.$$
In particular, each $V_i$ is invariant under every $\lambda \in \Lambda$.

**Descent to the quotient.** Since the $V_i$ are $\Lambda$-invariant holomorphic vector fields on $\mathbb{C}^n$, they descend to well-defined global holomorphic vector fields $\overline{V}_1, \dots, \overline{V}_n$ on $X$. Concretely, $\overline{V}_i([p])$ is defined as $d\pi_p(V_i(p))$, and $\Lambda$-invariance guarantees this is independent of the choice of representative $p$.

**They form a basis at every point.** At any $[p] \in X$, the projection $\pi$ is a local biholomorphism, so $d\pi_p : T_p\mathbb{C}^n \to T_{[p]}X$ is a linear isomorphism. Since $V_1(p), \dots, V_n(p) = \partial/\partial z_1|_p, \dots, \partial/\partial z_n|_p$ form a basis of $T_p\mathbb{C}^n$, their images
$$\overline{V}_1([p]), \dots, \overline{V}_n([p])$$
form a basis of $T_{[p]}X$.

**Conclusion.** The $\overline{V}_1, \dots, \overline{V}_n$ are $n$ holomorphic sections of the holomorphic tangent bundle $T_X$ that constitute a basis of the holomorphic tangent space at every point of $X$. This means $T_X \cong \mathcal{O}_X^{\oplus n}$ is holomorphically trivial, so $X$ is holomorphically parallelizable.

Therefore, for every $n \geq 1$, a compact complex manifold of complex dimension $n$ that is holomorphically parallelizable exists — namely, any $n$-dimensional complex torus $\mathbb{C}^n/\Lambda$. $\blacksquare$

